# 3. Drive it during the talk

A demo that only runs is a video. A demo you change in front of the audience explains: one parameter at a
time, the case that fails, the value that matters. This page connects your program to the slide with a
small bridge: two C++ files, the page of the demo (`slide-frame.html`) and a script for the slide
(`slide-controls.js`), in [the repository]({{ repo }}/tree/main/bridge). Nothing in it depends on a slide
tool: the slide side is plain HTML.

## Declare the parameters once

Add `slide_bridge.hpp` and `slide_bridge.cpp` to your sources, and declare each parameter the slide may
change, with its range or its choices:

```cpp
{% raw %}#include "slide_bridge.hpp"

int main(int argc, char* argv[])
{
    slide::parse(argc, argv);     // the --key value pairs: the address of the frame
    // ... create the window, initialize the scene ...
    slide::param("ampl", wave.amplitude, 0.0f, 0.3f, 0.01f).with_label("amplitude");
    slide::param("shape", wave.shape, {{"ripple", "Ripple"}, {"waves", "Waves"}});
    slide::param("wireframe", wave.wireframe);
    slide::action("drop", [&] { t = 0; });
    // ... main loop ...
}{% endraw %}
```

The program keeps reading its own variables: the bridge only writes them. When the stored value has other
units than the one you show (a frequency in Hz shown, 2&pi;f stored), declare it with a getter and a setter:

```cpp
slide::param("freq", [&] { return omega / (2 * pi); }, [&](float f) { omega = 2 * pi * f; }, 0.3f, 5.0f);
```

`on_change` runs code when the slide changes a value (reload a model, rebuild a mesh):
`slide::param("samples", grid.samples, 8, 100).on_change([&] { rebuild(); });`.

## A preset per slide: the address

The address of the frame gives the start values: `index.html?ampl=0.2&k=14` starts the program with
`--ampl 0.2 --k 14`. One build then serves several slides, each with its own preset:

```html
<iframe class="demo" src="demo/index.html?ampl=0.2&k=14"></iframe>
```

## A panel, built from the program

`controls=` lists the controls to show, in their order. The page builds them from the declarations: a
slider for a number, buttons for a choice, a toggle for a bool, a button for an action.

```html
<iframe class="demo" src="demo/index.html?controls=ampl,k,speed,wireframe&panel=bl"></iframe>
```

<div class="shots one"><figure class="figure"><a class="demo" data-label="Live" href="../../decks/lhtml-site/html/02_panel/index.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="../../assets/img/slide-program-panel.jpg" alt="A slide with a twisted box on the right and a panel of sliders over it" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption>A program in raw OpenGL 3.3: each slider changes a uniform while it runs.</figcaption></figure></div>

- `mode:lbs:drag` keeps only two buttons of the choice `mode`.
- `panel=tl`, `tr` (default), `bl` or `br` puts the panel in a corner.
- `compact=1` gives short labels and a small panel, for a small demo; on a full slide, keep the default,
  readable in the room.

## Buttons in the text of the slide

Load `slide-controls.js` in the slide, and write the buttons in HTML. `data-demo-set` gives the settings to
apply, `data-demo-value` shows a value live:

```html
<button data-demo-set="shape=ripple">Ripple</button>
<button data-demo-set="shape=waves">Waves</button>
<button data-demo-set="drop">Drop</button>
<button data-demo-set="ampl=0.25&k=14">Strong</button>
Amplitude: <span data-demo-value="ampl" data-digits="2"></span>

<script src="slide-controls.js"></script>
<style> button.on { background: #333; color: white; } </style>
```

- A button gets the class `on` while its settings are the current values of the demo: the audience sees
  which state is shown.
- `drop` has no `=`: it runs the action `drop`.
- The demo is the first `iframe.demo` of the page. In a tool that puts all the slides in one page
  (reveal.js), name it: `data-demo="#surface"` on the button, `id="surface"` on the frame.
- From a script: `slideDemo.set({ampl: 0.2})`, `slideDemo.params()`.

