Underneath, the slide and the demo exchange messages (`postMessage`), which also works when the demo is
hosted on another site: [the protocol](../../02_more/02_reference/index.html#messages).

## The ImGui window of your program

ImGui is the quickest interface to write, and it works in a frame as it is. Make it readable in the room
by scaling its text from the address (`?gui_scale=1.4`, with
`slide::param("gui_scale", io_font_scale, 0.5f, 3.0f)` and `ImGui::GetIO().FontGlobalScale = io_font_scale`
at each frame). Keep its sections closed at start: a long window goes out of the frame. The slide can
change the same variables: ImGui shows the new values at the next frame, as it reads them
([C6]({{ repo }}/tree/main/examples/c6-cgp-marching-cubes)).

## Values from the program to the slide

Some values are computed by the program: the length of a curve you drag, a number of triangles, a time.
Declare them as read only, and tell the page when the program changed them:

```cpp
slide::readout("length", [&] { return curve_length; });

// at each frame, once the curve is updated
if (std::abs(curve_length - shown_length) > 1e-4f) {
    shown_length = curve_length;
    slide::notify();
}
```

The slide shows it with `<span data-demo-value="length" data-digits="2"></span>`, and the panel with
`controls=length`. The page sends at most one update a frame ([C7]({{ repo }}/tree/main/examples/c7-cgp-drag-points)).

## The keys of the slides

Once you click in a demo, the keyboard goes to the frame, not to the slides: the arrow keys and the remote
control stop changing the slide. `slide-frame.html` fixes it: it sends the arrow keys and Page Down /
Page Up to the slides (and posts them when the demo comes from another site, for `slide-controls.js` to
replay). A demo that uses a key for itself calls `preventDefault()` on it, and keeps it. lhtml-site (after
1.1.0) listens to the keys of its demos itself, for any page.

